    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.47460/minerva.v7i19.284",
    "abstract": "This study analyzes the effects of climate change on the Andean shrubland ecosystem in the department of Lima (Peru) during the period 1970--2022. Climate data on temperature and precipitation (WorldClim) were used together with the Soil Adjusted Vegetation Index (SAVI) derived from Landsat imagery, complemented with community perception. The results reveal interdecadal thermal variability, irregular precipitation regimes, and a significant relationship between water availability and vegetation vigor. Periods of ecosystem degradation associated with drought events and high thermal amplitude were identified. The methodological triangulation confirms the high sensitivity of the Andean shrubland ecosystem to regional climatic variability.",
    "field": "Climate change impacts on ecosystems",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of quantifying the high sensitivity of the Andean shrubland ecosystem to climatic variability, where changes in temperature and precipitation cause significant vegetation degradation, thereby complicating the development of effective, evidence-based adaptation strategies.",
    "method": "This study employs a correlational analysis combining historical climate data (WorldClim), a satellite-derived vegetation index (SAVI), and community perception surveys to assess the effects of climate variability on the Andean shrubland ecosystem in Lima, Peru.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze the effects of climate change on the Andean shrubland ecosystem in Lima, Peru, from 1970 to 2022, using climatic data, the SAVI vegetation index from Landsat imagery, and community perception.",
